This paper investigates scattering in cubic inhomogeneous Schrödinger equations where initial conditions have limited average, demonstrating the use of optimal estimates in both space and frequency analysis to establish the scattering condition. Specifically, in the focusing cubic InSE, we show that initial data with integrated square-integrability or bounded variation lead to scattering, whereas in the defocusing cubic InSE, we show that initial data with square-integrability or bounded variation greater than negative one-half lead to scattering. These results surpass previous constraints on variational or squared variational conditions for both models. Furthermore, in the case of radial data for the defocusing cubic InSE, we show that initial data with Var$^{\frac{1}{4}}_2(v_0)$ or Squ$^{\frac{1}{2}}_2(v_0)$ lead to scattering, thereby improving previous estimates for this model. In doing so, this paper establishes the following diagram of conditions for both models:
